# The standard-library modules every Kex source file sees without a +using+.
#
# The prelude holds everyday values and operations (+List+, +Map+, +String+,
# +Math+, +IO+), the protocols the language itself relies on (+Enumerable+,
# +Truthyable+, +Errorable+), and core runtime services such as processes and
# time. That is why +.map+, conditions and +.try+ work without importing the
# traits that define them.
#
# Every prelude name is in scope in every program, so a module belongs here
# only when most code needs it or the language depends on it. A module stays
# opt-in when it grants a capability that should show up in a file's imports,
# or when most programs have no use for its names. The filesystem (+FS+),
# regular expressions (+Regex+), JSON, parser combinators (+Parsing+), bit
# operations (+Bits+), data structures such as +Data.Set+, hashing (+Digest+),
# command-line parsing (+OptionParser+) and the sandboxed evaluator
# (+Evaluator+) are all opt-in.
#
#   using FS
#
#   main do
#     let config = FS.File.read("config.txt").or("")
#     IO.printLine(config)
#   end
#
# This file only lists modules. Each bare +using+ names a sibling stdlib
# source, and the toolchain expands them in order when it builds or loads the
# prelude. Document a type or function next to its declaration in that source,
# not here.

using Algebra
